{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>I, Qohelet.</b> The subject matter of the book indicates that he composed it at the end of his days, as if to say to future generations: thus and thus I tested in the days of my life, and I was able to test everything because I was king. And the meaning of 'over Israel' is because among them all were prophets and wise-hearted men, like the sons of Zerah, and not like the sons of Kedar who dwell in tents. And the point is that he was king over a wise and understanding people. And he said 'in Jerusalem' because the place is suited to receive wisdom, for it is known that the inhabited world is divided into seven parts, and it is impossible for upright men to receive wisdom except in the three middle parts, for in the first and last parts the excess of heat or cold prevents a sound human disposition from arising. And it is known that the latitude of Jerusalem is thirty-three degrees, and this is half the inhabited world, for habitation is possible only when the sun inclines [a certain number of] degrees north or south."], :book :ecclesiastes, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {0 "The final clause reads 'אחר מעלות' literally 'after degrees,' which may be corrupt or elliptical; translated hyper-literally."}, :source-witnesses [["Wikisource" "https://he.wikisource.org/wiki/%D7%90%D7%91%D7%9F_%D7%A2%D7%96%D7%A8%D7%90_%D7%A2%D7%9C_%D7%A7%D7%94%D7%9C%D7%AA"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:26:49.153187384Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}